format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-meets-matter

lesson detecting-radiation
title: Detecting radiation
minutes: 6
xp: 50
teaches: u3-detectors
teaches: u3-background-reading
relies_on: u3-ionizing-meaning

description:
    Radiation is detected and measured with instruments. You will find out why a detector reads something almost anywhere, and how a Geiger counter works.

cite ref-openstax-chem-21-6

supports:
    A Geiger counter detects the ionization of the gas in its tube; a scintillation counter uses a material that emits light when ionizing radiation excites it; dosimeters measure ionizing radiation and are often used to determine personal radiation exposure (the page's picture shows one worn on the chest).

cite ref-epa-radiation-basics

supports:
    Radiation is present all around us; background radiation comes mostly from natural minerals in the ground, soil and water, and from cosmic radiation.

cite ref-who-ionizing-radiation
supports: Humans are exposed to natural radiation sources every day, and the level varies from place to place.

card c01
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: A puzzle in an empty field

body:
    Here is a puzzle. You switch on a radiation detector in an empty field. Nothing radioactive is anywhere near. The detector still shows a small reading. Is it broken?

card c02
kind: item
type: item
item: u3-l4-predict-room
scored: 0

card c03
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Background radiation
figure: always-something
figure_at: 2

body:
    [[background-radiation|Background radiation]] is present on Earth at all times. It comes mostly from natural minerals in the ground, soil and water, and from cosmic radiation. So a working detector picks up a little almost anywhere.

card c04
kind: item
type: item
item: u3-l4-choice-background
connected: true

card c05
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Inside a Geiger counter
figure: geiger-tube
figure_at: 2

body:
    A [[geiger-counter|Geiger counter]] has a tube of gas. When radiation passes through, it ionizes the gas. The counter detects that ionization.

card c06
kind: item
type: item
item: u3-l4-order-geiger
connected: true

card c07
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: More radiation, more counts
figure: more-radiation-more-counts
figure_at: 1

body:
    The rate of ionization goes with the amount of radiation. A little radiation gives a few counts, and more radiation gives many more. Background radiation varies from place to place, so the reading does too.

